WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:01.099
I've

00:00:01.099 --> 00:00:05.819
said a lot of times, I don't remember the
number that we just can't afford to wait until

00:00:05.819 --> 00:00:11.610
the week before a major election to call out
voter suppression and voter purges.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:13.200
And I'm trying to make good on that.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:18.760
And today we're 10 and a half, 11 months out
from the election, and we have news once again

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:24.780
that Georgia Republicans are purging hundreds
of thousands of voters from the voter rolls.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.230
They did this in the lead up to the 2018 election
in Georgia.

00:00:28.230 --> 00:00:30.300
You might remember this at least played a
role.

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:35.179
I won't say it was the factor that denied
democratic gubernatorial candidate Stacey

00:00:35.179 --> 00:00:40.569
Abrams the win, but it played a role in her
not winning that election.

00:00:40.569 --> 00:00:47.329
And once again, we have the Georgia, a Republican
secretary of state, Brad Reffind, [inaudible]

00:00:47.329 --> 00:00:52.670
purging voters by the hundreds of thousands
and a federal judge has decided this week

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:58.420
that they can go ahead and remove 300,000
voters that they say are inactive.

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:03.819
Now, of course you might say, well, you know,
inactive sounds like dead people.

00:01:03.819 --> 00:01:08.490
People who move to other States and are voting
elsewhere, they should be removed from voter

00:01:08.490 --> 00:01:09.659
rolls, right?

00:01:09.659 --> 00:01:11.170
Very, very wrong.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:17.030
More than 120,000 of those 300,000 people
who are being removed are being removed merely

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:19.320
because they've not voted for a while.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:24.750
This is not a good reason to remove them,
and this will lead to people who do want to

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:30.930
vote in November who are notified about their
removal at an old address and they might want

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.530
to vote to remove the remove Donald Trump
in November and they will show up and be told,

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:38.530
sorry, you're not registered in Georgia.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:45.630
You are allowed to register to vote only until
the fifth Monday before election day.

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.890
That means if you don't realize you've been
purged until election day, you're out of luck

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.689
and you're not going to be able to vote.

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:53.890
Now let's look at the numbers.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:55.219
A little bit.

00:01:55.219 --> 00:02:00.719
300,000 voters, that's 4% of the Georgia electorate.

00:02:00.719 --> 00:02:07.470
In 2016 Donald Trump won in Georgia 50 to
45 that's five points.

00:02:07.470 --> 00:02:12.940
If it's closer to this time around, it might
only be a two or three point margin in Georgia,

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:18.360
and we are talking about removing 4% of the
electorate by purging them due to inactivity.

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:26.420
A bad faith actor could strategically purge
only so-called inactive voters that they determine

00:02:26.420 --> 00:02:30.960
based on geography or for other reasons, are
more likely to be democratic voters rather

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.720
than Republican voters and could at least
hypothetically swing an election with these

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.160
voter purges.

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:38.780
Meanwhile, it's not just Georgia.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:40.810
I know this is happening in a lot of States
today.

00:02:40.810 --> 00:02:41.810
All high.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.550
I've highlighted Georgia already.

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:45.480
